</mjx-container>, which yields Theorem 4.4.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The paper does not introduce a new method, but mainly validates the effe=
ctiveness of combining LoRA with different variants of SAM.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The contribution of the paper is analytical rather than algorithmic. It =
resolves an open PECL design question, <strong>under subspace-constrained u=
pdates, which perturbation scope and geometry are meaningful, and why flat-=
minima methods can remain effective despite extreme parameter constraints</=
strong>.</p>
<p>Theorem 4.4 is the core result: it shows that the bound-relevant sharpne=
ss and complexity reduce to quantities supported on the admissible adapter =
subspace, which explains why perturbations outside this subspace are misali=
gned in PECL.</p>
<p>Our experiments support this prediction in two ways. First, full-scope p=
erturbations consistently underperform adapter-scoped perturbations, confir=
ming that probing frozen directions is misaligned with the PECL adaptation =
space. Second, within the correct adapter scope, we observe a stable orderi=
ng across perturbation geometries, with RS(0) &lt; AS(0) &lt; AS(1) in both=
 curvature and accuracy.</p>
<p>The contribution is therefore not a new optimizer, but a principled expl=
anation of which sharpness object matters in LoRA-based PECL and how pertur=
bation design should match the admissible update space.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Could the authors provide experimental results at a larger scale and und=
er more diverse settings?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To address this concern, we add results in three settings:</p>
<ol>
<li>five fine-grained CL benchmarks under the same ViT-B/16 setup</li>
<li>a 5-task cross-dataset stream where each task comes from a different fi=
ne-grained domain</li>
<li>standard NLP CL benchmarks with T5-small and T5-large.</li>
</ol>
<p>We refer the reviewer to our response to reviewer <strong>mBoF</strong> =
for the full numerical results.</p>
<p>[1] Flat-LoRA: Low-Rank Adapation over a Flat Loss Landscape, ICML 2025<=
/p>
<p>[2] A PAC-Bayesian Bound for Lifelong Learning</p>
