rong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>Dear AC,</p>
<p>We sincerely appreciate the time and care that you and the reviewers hav=
e devoted to evaluating our submission, as well as the thoughtful discussio=
n throughout the rebuttal process.</p>
<p>We write only to clarify what seems to be the central source of the rema=
ining disagreement. Reviewer TSrA questions whether full-parameter perturba=
tion is a natural PECL setting (<strong>TSrA Cons 1</strong>), and Reviewer=
 Hwkz similarly treats adapter-only perturbation as the standard LoRA setti=
ng (<strong>Hwkz Weaknesses 1</strong>). This concern seems to underlie the=
ir later view that the paper's motivation remains insufficiently justified =
(<strong>TSrA Ack, Hwkz Ack</strong>). Our paper, however, is not intended =
as an argument about which SAM implementation is more standard or preferabl=
e in practice. It addresses a prior theory-level question: which sharpness =
object is actually relevant in frozen-backbone PECL?</p>
<p>We view this question as fundamental because sharpness is already centra=
l to generalization and forgetting, while PECL changes the admissible geome=
try of future adaptation. Once updates are restricted to a low-rank admissi=
ble subspace, the flatness notion inherited from full-parameter training is=
 no longer automatically the right one. Until this point is clarified, flat=
ness-based PECL remains partly heuristic, since it is unclear what should b=
e perturbed and which geometry is actually relevant. Theorem 4.4 is meant t=
o resolve precisely this issue by showing that, after specializing the sequ=
ential PAC-Bayesian analysis to frozen-backbone PECL, the bound-relevant sh=
arpness term and the KL complexity are determined by the admissible update =
space.</p>
<p>We therefore respectfully ask that the paper be evaluated as a theory-dr=
iven analysis of the relevant sharpness object in PECL, rather than as an e=
mpirical recommendation about default SAM implementations. We hope this cla=
rification can be taken into account in the final assessment.</p>
<p>Best regards,</p>
<p>The authors</p>
