ote-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Summary:</strong> <div class=3D"no=
te-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>This paper studies sharpness-aware o=
ptimization in the context of parameter-efficient continual learning (PECL)=
 with LoRA adapters. The authors derive a sequential hierarchical PAC-Bayes=
ian bound showing that both the sharpness penalty and KL complexity terms r=
educe to quantities defined on the task-permissible LoRA subspace. They fur=
ther argue that perturbations should be restricted to this subspace, and th=
at first-order adversarial sharpness (AS(1)) most effectively suppresses do=
minant curvature directions within it. Experiments on ImageNet-R, ImageNet-=
C, and ImageNet-P support these claims across three LoRA organizational str=
ategies.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Strengths And Weaknesses:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdo=
wn-rendered"><p>Soundness. The theoretical framework is generally well-moti=
vated. Lemma 4.2's translation-invariance argument is sound, and the KL dec=
omposition onto the subspace is clean. The experiments are also reasonable =
and show convincing results.
Presentation. The paper is clearly written and the progression from theory =
to experiments is logical. The paper is also appropriately referenced. Howe=
ver, the three sharpness types (RS(0), AS(0), AS(1)) are introduced purely =
through equations, and the paper would benefit from an intuitive figure ill=
ustrating their geometric differences - particularly since the performance =
ordering maps directly onto the sharpness strength ordering, which is the p=
aper's central empirical finding. Figure 4 is useful but arrives late and i=
s too technical to serve this purpose.
Significance. The scope mismatch finding - that perturbing frozen backbone =
parameters hurts rather than helps - is a practically important and non-obv=
ious result for practitioners using SAM-style methods with LoRA. The consis=
tent advantage of AS(1) across all settings is also a useful empirical find=
ing.
Originality. The extension of hierarchical PAC-Bayes to a sequentially evol=
ving hyperposterior with an explicit drift penalty seems like a genuine con=
tribution. The combination of this framework with LoRA-specific structure h=
as not been done before to the reviewer's knowledge.</p>
