ote-content-container "><div class=3D"note-content"><div><strong class=3D"n=
ote-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Summary:</strong> <div class=3D"no=
te-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>This paper investigates the relation=
ship between subspace sharpness and generalization in the context of Low-Ra=
nk Adaptation (LoRA) for Parameter-Efficient Continual Learning (PECL).
While sharpness-aware optimization is a known catalyst for generalization i=
n full-model training, the authors explore whether these benefits translate=
 to the low-rank, task-specific subspaces used by LoRA.
Curvature diagnostics show lower subspace sharpness is related to higher co=
ntinual accuracy and less forgetting across tasks.</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Strengths And Weaknesses:</strong> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdo=
wn-rendered"><p>[Strengths]</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Providing theoretical justification for sharpness analysis in the PE=
CL domain.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Clearly well written.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>[Weaknesses]</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Limited generalization: The current experiments only use a ViT-B/16 =
model pretrained on ImageNet-21K. In addition, the continual learning datas=
ets are also limited to ImageNet-C/-R/-P. Since the evaluation relies on a =
single model type and a single dataset domain, the empirical evidence is no=
t sufficient to support the claimed generalization.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Limited scalability: Does this analysis hold regardless of model com=
plexity or model size?</p>
</li>
<li><p>Overall, the observations and insights appear somewhat incremental (=
just very similar to full-fine tuning).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
>Soundness:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">2: fair</span></div=
><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Presentati=
on:</strong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">3: good</span></div><div><s=
trong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Significance:</str=
ong> <span class=3D"note-content-value">2: fair</span></div><div><strong cl=
ass=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Originality:</strong> <spa=
n class=3D"note-content-value">2: fair</span></div><div><strong class=3D"no=
te-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Key Questions For Authors:</strong>=
 <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><ul>
<li><p>FlatLoRA [1] highlights that LoRA fine-tuning can lead to sharper lo=
ss landscapes in the full parameter space and proposes encouraging flatter =
minima during LoRA fine-tuning with their objective. Do the authors think t=
heir approach would influence the generalization bound derived in this work=
?</p>
</li>
<li><p>Could the authors provide additional empirical data covering various=
 model architectures and scales?</p>
</li>
<li><p>Can the authors provide more empirical analysis using different data=
sets, including cross-dataset scenarios?</p>
</li>
<li><p>Is this approach affected by the domain gap between the pre-training=
 dataset and the target dataset used for continual learning?</p>
</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Reference</li>
</ul>
<p>[1] Flat-LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation over a Flat Loss Landscape (ICML 2025=
)</p>
</div></div><div><strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering"=
