<blockquote>
<p>FlatLoRA highlights that LoRA fine-tuning can lead to sharper loss lands=
capes in the full parameter space and proposes encouraging flatter minima d=
uring LoRA fine-tuning with their objective. Do the authors think their app=
roach would influence the generalization bound derived in this work?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As long as the posterior remains supported on the admissible update spac=
e, Flat-LoRA [2] would not change the form of our bound; it can influence i=
t only indirectly through the solution it learns. Our analysis does not dep=
end on optimizing a particular flatness objective, but on which sharpness n=
otion enters the frozen-backbone PECL bound. Under this support condition, =
Theorem 4.1 still applies, and Theorem 4.4 shows that both the KL term and =
the sharpness term reduce to quantities supported on <mjx-container class=
