<strong class=3D"note-content-field disable-tex-rendering">Comment:</strong=
> <div class=3D"note-content-value markdown-rendered"><p>Thank you for the =
follow-up. </p>
<p>Our claim is not architecture-wise universality, but a structural one: u=
nder frozen-backbone PECL, once future adaptation is confined to an admissi=
ble low-rank update subspace, the sharpness object relevant to continual ge=
neralization changes accordingly. To broaden the empirical scope, we added =
decoder-only LLM results on Llama-3.2-1B and 3B, extending the earlier ViT =
and T5 evidence to a new model family and larger scales. The effect remains=
 directionally consistent across all settings, which we view as sufficient =
support for the paper=E2=80=99s qualitative claim.</p>
<p>More importantly, the contribution is not merely incremental: the unreso=
lved issue is PECL-specific. The known point is that flatness may help gene=
ralization in full-model training or standard LoRA fine-tuning. The unresol=
ved point is PECL-specific: under frozen-backbone, subspace-constrained ada=
ptation, it remained unclear which notion of sharpness is theoretically rel=
evant, why full-space and subspace perturbations may yield different conclu=
sions, and how flat-minima methods should be interpreted under severe updat=
e constraints. Without resolving this issue, subsequent PECL work can only =
import flatness heuristically. Our contribution is to provide the missing c=
riterion by identifying the PECL-relevant sharpness object, thereby making =
later flatness-based PECL analysis and method design more principled and in=
terpretable.</p>
