MathML"><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow data-mjx-texclass=3D"ORD"><mi mathvariant=3D"=
normal">=CE=94</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></math></mjx-assistive=
-mml></mjx-container>. This is the source of the scope misalignment: full-s=
cope perturbations probe curvature in frozen directions that the learner ca=
nnot use, so they do not correspond to the sharpness notion relevant to PEC=
L adaptation. Within the correct scope, perturbation geometry should still =
matter; Table 1 and Fig. 4 show RS(0) &lt; AS(0) &lt; AS(1), consistent wit=
h better curvature alignment. Additional experiments on five fine-grained d=
atasets (see our response to reviewer <strong>mBoF</strong>) show the same =
trend.</p>
<p>Our contribution is therefore not simply the observation that =E2=80=9Ca=
dapter-only SAM works,=E2=80=9D but a PECL-grounded criterion explaining wh=
y adapter-restricted sharpness is the relevant object under frozen-backbone=
 adaptation and why full-scope perturbations are misaligned.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The paper should discuss prior work that applies PAC-Bayesian analysis t=
o continual learning.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Thanks. We will clarify in the revision how our result relates to [3] an=
d [4]. Paper [3] studies a lifelong-learning setting where tasks are modele=
d as i.i.d. draws from a fixed hyperdistribution, yielding an order-invaria=
nt bound. Our framework instead uses a filtration-adapted hyperposterior pr=
ocess <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" ta=
bindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"43" style=3D"font-size: 121.3%; position: =
